  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDry and quiet weather is expected today into Monday afternoon as\nhigh pressure remains in control. Confidence is high that\ntemperatures will rise to 5 to 10 degrees above climatological\naverages, although record highs are not anticipated.\n\nA passing shortwave disturbance may trigger isolated\nthunderstorm development Monday evening across central Ohio.\nHigh-resolution guidance indicates that the greatest storm\ncoverage will remain near Columbus and Cincinnati areas.\nHowever, a few isolated single cell storms could develop towards\nZanesville and Coshocton region. Should this occur, localized\ndownburst winds would be possible due to a combination of modest\ninstability and weak vertical shear. Forecast surface-based CAPE\nvalues between 1200-1900J/kg support the potential for a few\nstorms to produce isolated damaging wind gusts. Across northern\nWest Virginia and western Pennsylvania, warm air in the mid-\nlevels of the atmosphere (around 700mb) could inhibit updraft\ngrowth, reducing the potential for thunderstorm development. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nThe combination of a moisture-rich airmass, above-average \ntemperatures, and weaken ridge pattern is expected to increase \nthe risk for severe thunderstorms and heavy rainfall during the \nmiddle of the week. Any stronger storms could produce a range of\nhazards, including damaging wind gusts, large hail, tornadoes, \nand localized flooding. \n\nMachine-learning guidance currently indicates a 30-40%\nprobability of severe weather impacts across the Ohio River\nValley Wednesday into Friday that will need to be heavily\nmonitored. \n\nThere is also a potential for near-record to record warmth\nstarting Tuesday, with both record high temperatures and\nrecord-high minimum temperatures at night. However, the\ndevelopment of thunderstorms associated cloud coverage and\ncooling precip could ultimate reduce the likelihood of reaching\nthese values, especially the record high temperatures.",
